WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.061
A constraint restricts which directions are available

00:00:03.061 --> 00:00:14.467
To minimize squared distance from the origin while staying on the line x plus y equals three, we must search along that line, rather than move freely toward the origin.

00:00:15.767 --> 00:00:21.567
At a regular constrained extremum, the objective gradient is parallel to the constraint gradient

00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:25.295
Otherwise a tangent direction would still decrease the objective

00:00:25.295 --> 00:00:33.167
Here the gradient equations give x equals y; combining that with the constraint gives one and a half each.

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:36.539
Lagrange multipliers produced a candidate

00:00:36.539 --> 00:00:45.656
Substitution now proves it is the global minimum: the squared term is always nonnegative, and vanishes exactly at one and a half

00:00:45.656 --> 00:00:49.800
The minimum squared distance is therefore four and a half.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:56.641
The usual multiplier condition needs regularity, such as a nonzero constraint gradient

00:00:56.641 --> 00:01:04.954
The constraint x squared plus y squared equals zero has only one feasible point, where its gradient vanishes

00:01:04.954 --> 00:01:10.033
That point is optimal even for objectives whose gradients are nonzero.
